Negative control for the listing's ACTIVE/CLOSED status filter (AC1/AC2).

Points the CLOSED position at the `neq` operator — the ACTIVE position's — so
both narrow the collection the same way. The control still draws all three
positions, still writes through `setCriteria`, and still puts a `status.code`
filter on the wire: only the OPERATOR is wrong, which is the whole of what
separates "my closed tickets" from "my open ones". A spec that finds the
control, or reads only that some status filter left, stays green through it.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: `client-tickets-listing.spec.ts` — "AC2 Closed puts
filter[status.code]=ticket_closed on the wire, and nothing beside it" (its own
assertion), plus the two cases that click Closed on their way through: the swap
case and "All clears the narrowing".

diff --git a/packages/headless/src/modules/tickets/tickets.services.ts b/packages/headless/src/modules/tickets/tickets.services.ts
index 22fe017..2000867 100644
--- a/packages/headless/src/modules/tickets/tickets.services.ts
+++ b/packages/headless/src/modules/tickets/tickets.services.ts
@@ -182,7 +182,7 @@ function applyStatusCodeFilter(url: URL, isClosed: boolean | null): void {
   url.searchParams.delete("filter[status.code|neq]");
 
   if (isClosed === true)
-    url.searchParams.set("filter[status.code]", TicketStatusCodes.CLOSED);
+    url.searchParams.set("filter[status.code|neq]", TicketStatusCodes.CLOSED);
   else if (isClosed === false)
     url.searchParams.set("filter[status.code|neq]", TicketStatusCodes.CLOSED);
 }
